===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|coat}}
{{inh+|en|enm|jakke}}, from {{der|en|xno|jacke}}, {{der|en|frm|jaque}}, {{m|fr|jacque}}, from {{m|frm|jacques||peasant}}, from the proper name {{m|fr|Jacques}}. Compare {{m|fr|jacquerie}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[coarse]] [[mediaeval]] [[coat]] of [[defence]], especially one made of [[leather]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: ''{{w|jack of plate}}''  (armor made up of small metal plates sewn between layers of cloth, similar to a [[brigandine]])
#: ''jack of mail''
#: ''padded jack''
#* '''1591''', John Harington, translating Ariosto's ''Orlando Furioso'', x. 73 (quoted in e.g. '''1822''', Robert Nares, ''A Glossary'', page 186):
#*: Their horsemen are with '''jacks''' for most part clad, / Their horses are both swift of course and strong, / They run on horseback with a slender gad, / And like a speare, but that it is more long.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1766|author={{w|Walter Harris (historian)|Walter Harris}}|title=The history and antiquities of the city of Dublin
|passage=threescore men in '''jacks''' or light coats of mail}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1786| passage=The aketon, gambeson, vambasium, and '''jack''' were military vestments, calculated for the defence of the body, differing little from each other, except in their names, their materials and construction were nearly the same, the authorities quoted in the notes, shew they were all composed of many folds of linen, stuffed with cotton, wool or hair, quilted, and commonly covered with leather, made of buck or doe skin.| author=Francis Grose| title=A Treatise on Ancient Armour and Weapons| page=15}}

