## Any of the marine fish in the family {{taxfmt|Carangidae|family}}. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|jack mackerel}}
## {{lb|en|US}} A [[jackrabbit]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1932|author=Isabel T. Kelly|title=Ethnography of the Surprise Valley Paiute|journal=University of California Publications in California Archaeology an Ethnography|volume=31|number=3|page=88|text=Cottontails were taken along the creeks, under the willows. Their flesh was preferable to that of the jacks{{...}}"}}
## A large California [[rockfish]], the [[bocaccio]], {{taxlink|Sebastes paucispinis|species}}.
## {{taxlink|Mangifera caesia|species}}, related to the [[mango]] tree.
## {{c|en|Arum family plants}}{{lb|en|colloquial}} Plant in the genus {{taxfmt|Arisaema|genus}}, also known as [[Jack-in-the-pulpit]], and capitalized [[Jack]].
##* {{quote-web|en|work=Hilton Pond Center for Piedmont Natural History|date=2003-05-01|url=http://www.hiltonpond.org/ThisWeek030501.html|title=Is that “Jack” in the Pulpit|passage=Usually a '''jack''' that makes male flowers has only one main leaf (right), while female plants have two. […] The specific taxonomy of Jack-in-the Pulpit, a member of the Arum Family (Araceae), is rather up in the air. Some botanists believe all '''jacks''' are just one species, ''Arisaema triphyllum'', while others claim there are as many as three: ''A. triphyllum'', ''A. atrorubens'', and ''A. stewardsonii''.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|date=a. 06-05-2013|url=http://www.eattheweeds.com/arisaema-triphyllum-jack-and-jill-and-no-hill-2/|title=Jack-in-the-Pulpit, and Jill|work=Eat the Weeds|passage=In fact, most male '''Jacks''' are under 14 inches tall. Most '''Jacks''' over 14 inches tend to be Jills.}}
## {{lb|en|colloquial}} [[spadix|Spadix]] of a plant (also capitalized [[Jack]]).
##* {{quote-web|en|work=Hilton Pond Center for Piedmont Natural History|date=2003-05-01|url=http://www.hiltonpond.org/ThisWeek030501.html|title=Is that “Jack” in the Pulpit|passage=Lifting the flap at the top of the spathe reveals our slender and round-headed friend "'''Jack'''," known better to botanists as the spadix.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|date=24-05-2017|author=Stephen Westcott-Gratton|work=Gardenmaking|title=Purple pulpits and trilliums|url=https://gardenmaking.com/jack-in-the-pulpit-trilliums/|passage=On every kid’s list of favourite plants is our quirky Jack-in-the-pulpit with its green, red or purple spadices (the '''Jacks''') and hooded green-, red- or almost black-striped spathes (the pulpits).}}
## {{c|en|Buckwheat family plants}} {{lb|en|apparently does not occur standalone for the genus per se}} Plant of the genus {{taxfmt|Emex|genus}}, also considered synonymous to {{taxfmt|Rumex|genus}}, if not then containing two species {{vern|lesser jack}} and {{vern|little jack}} for {{taxlink|Emex spinosa|species}} syn. {{taxlink|Rumex spinosus|species}}, Australian English {{vern|three-corner jack}} and {{vern|prickly jack}} for {{taxlink|Emex australis|species}} syn. {{taxlink|Rumex hypogaeus|species}}.

